course: dictionary
lesson: 850
title: Name, letter or clue?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words look ordinary but change their job depending on the sentence. The clues are the words around them. Beth is a good example: it can be a person's name, a Hebrew letter, or a small part of a place name.
explain: «In Bangor, North Wales, both Mum and Dad come to every one of Bethan's netball matches and shout «Go on, Beth!» The cheering, the netball and the name Bethan tell you that Beth is a short, friendly name for a player.
explain: Ask yourself: does the sentence fit a person, a place or a letter? If Beth is shouted at a game, it is a name. If it sits in a list of alphabet letters, it is the Hebrew letter. A capital B at the start of a name is a strong clue.
fact: Beth is a girl's name, a short form of Elizabeth or Bethany.
fact: Beth, also spelled bet, is the second letter of the Hebrew alphabet.
fact: In some place names, beth means «house», as in Bethlehem, «house of bread».
fact: The Hebrew letter is not an everyday English word, so look at the context to decide what it means.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: In «Go on, Beth!», who is Beth most likely to be? | A letter of the alphabet | *A girl's name, short for Bethan | A town in Wales | A netball ball
exercise: In the place name Bethlehem, what does beth mean? | *House | River | Bread | Star
exercise: Beth is the second letter of which alphabet? | Greek | Arabic | Latin | *Hebrew
test: Which word is a short form of Elizabeth? | Bethlehem | Netball | Hebrew | *Beth
test: In the place name Bethel, what does beth mean? | Bread | *House | Night | Water
exam: A story says, «Beth walked to school with her friend.» What is Beth most likely to be? | A netball match | A letter of the Hebrew alphabet | A town in Wales | *A girl's name
